    "result_text": "The change is implemented and the full test suite is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nAdded a new create-owner rule: reject with **409** when the owner's city already contains 50 or more owners.\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions (mirroring the existing `CheckTelephoneUnique` / `CheckHouseholdUnique` steps), I added:\n\n1. **`CheckCityCapacity.java`** \u2014 a step function that counts persisted owners matching the request's city (case-insensitively, matching how `AssignCustomerCode` counts a city) and throws `CityAtCapacityException` when the count is \u2265 50. It reads `@Val OwnerFieldsDto` and runs under `govern: [ transaction ]`, so the count and insert see one consistent view and exclude the not-yet-saved owner.\n\n2. **`CityAtCapacityException.java`** \u2014 a checked exception (`extends Exception`).\n\n3. **`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 responds 409 with a `ProblemDetail`, identical in shape to the other duplicate handlers.\n\n4. **Escalation YAML** \u2014 `.../escalation/\u2026CityAtCapacityException.yml` wiring the exception to the handler.\n\n5. **Wired the step** into `owners.POST.yml` as `checkCityCapacity`, inserted after `checkHouseholdUnique` and before `build`, all within the write transaction.\n\n## Verification\n\n- New test `Cp18Tests.coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` (fill a city to 50, expect the 51st to 409): **passes**.\n- Full suite: **0 failures, 0 errors, 0 skipped** \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
